00:00:21.328 --> 00:00:24.413
When we talked about 'List'
so we also talked about methods of 'Lists'

00:00:24.438 --> 00:00:26.805
We learned how we can manipulate 'Lists'

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.800
Can we manipulate 'Tuple'?

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.019
Not in a direct way

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.889
If you want to change, add or remove
a value from a 'Tuple'

00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:36.812
So there's no direct method to do that

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:40.828
You have to copy it and make a list then
you have to do changes in that list

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.670
And then you have to change
it again in 'Tuple'

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:45.665
Because that's the main point of 'Tuple'
You cannot change it

00:00:45.713 --> 00:00:50.473
If a 'Tuple' contains 5 items so they'll remain 5
you cannot add 6ᵗʰ item in it

00:00:50.754 --> 00:00:57.782
But we'll now have a look on methods which will tell you about the
elements of a 'Tuple' or give you more information about it

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.943
As we learned
" 'Tuples' are immutable "

00:01:00.194 --> 00:01:05.975
" If you want to add, remove or change tuple items,
then first you must convert the tuple to a list. "

00:01:06.341 --> 00:01:11.207
And then you can perform functions like
'adding' 'removing' or 'changing'

00:01:11.395 --> 00:01:14.668
And again you can convert that
'List' into a 'Tuple'

00:01:14.916 --> 00:01:18.843
I have taken a quite good example here
And I want you all to have a look on

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.937
Here's a 'Tuple' and
I've created a temporary 'List'

00:01:22.994 --> 00:01:24.927
And then I appended 'Russia' in it

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:28.355
And then I've used a " .pop " method of 'List'

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:33.447
Then I made some changes in that 'List' and
finally I converted that 'List' into a 'Tuple'

00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:37.224
So this is how you can change a 'Tuple'
indirectly, but not directly

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:38.587
Remember this

00:01:38.994 --> 00:01:43.824
Now look, " Thus, we convert the tuple to a list,
manipulate items, then convert it back to a tuple. "

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:48.178
" However, we can directly concatenate two
tuples without converting them to list. "

00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:49.431
Because we're creating a new 'Tuple'

00:01:49.457 --> 00:01:51.477
We are not changing an existing 'Tuple'

00:01:51.626 --> 00:01:52.886
But we're creating a new 'Tuple'

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:58.284
When we are concatenating two 'Tuple'
we're creating a new 'Tuple'

00:01:58.684 --> 00:02:00.344
Now if you have a look on this example

00:02:01.374 --> 00:02:04.080
Because these example are already here
so I'm over-writing them

00:02:04.091 --> 00:02:06.096
You can copy &amp; run them on your own

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.699
So we've merged them both

00:02:09.724 --> 00:02:14.716
A new 'Tuple' has been created by merging the
names of these countries and these countries

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:16.877
This is how we can do this

00:02:17.104 --> 00:02:19.824
Now there are some methods of
'Tuple' which you must know

00:02:20.096 --> 00:02:27.108
For eg. there's a " .count " method which shows
the number of occurrences of a given item

00:02:27.196 --> 00:02:29.863
Now look I've created a 'Tuple'
with name " tuple1 "

00:02:29.888 --> 00:02:32.787
Let me make it small because we
use 'Capital' for classes

00:02:33.374 --> 00:02:36.721
This not important for now but you'll know this
in 'Object oriented programming'

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.907
I'll change this to small (t)

00:02:39.144 --> 00:02:44.266
There will be no difference but again it is a convention that
we use "Classes are written with capital letter"

00:02:44.676 --> 00:02:48.009
And variables are written with 'Small' letters

00:02:48.317 --> 00:02:52.350
3 occurs thrice
that's why I got 3 as output

00:02:53.064 --> 00:02:55.104
" tuple1.count(3) "

00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:58.151
The output is 3 and if I add another 3
so the output will be 4

00:02:58.176 --> 00:03:03.994
It'll show the number of occurrences
of a given item

00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:05.633
As simple as that

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:07.713
I hope you understood this

00:03:07.941 --> 00:03:14.377
Now " .index " method it is the same as 'List.index'
"Returns the first occurrence of the given element from 'Tuple' "

00:03:14.425 --> 00:03:19.499
It raise value error if the element is
not present in the list

00:03:19.524 --> 00:03:20.830
Remember this

00:03:21.307 --> 00:03:22.507
If I do something like this...

00:03:23.064 --> 00:03:28.210
If I write " res = tuple1.index(3) "

00:03:28.245 --> 00:03:32.920
We'll get the index of first occurrence of (3)
which is 3

00:03:33.271 --> 00:03:37.164
What if I change this to (31)?
So I'll get " 5 "

00:03:37.805 --> 00:03:41.131
Because it's on the fifth index

00:03:41.473 --> 00:03:43.199
This is what you must know

00:03:43.239 --> 00:03:44.252
Now there's another thing in this....

00:03:44.559 --> 00:03:46.945
I got the occurrence of (3)

00:03:47.398 --> 00:03:49.951
As I know there are a lot of
occurrence of (3) here

00:03:50.244 --> 00:03:53.338
But I want to know the index of (3)
from just this portion

00:03:53.406 --> 00:03:56.052
I can do it something like this

00:03:56.358 --> 00:03:58.011
That I can add starting point

00:03:58.346 --> 00:04:01.280
[0] [1] [2] [3] Let's suppose if I give 3
as starting point

00:04:01.805 --> 00:04:07.291
And then if I add ending point
[0] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7]

00:04:07.438 --> 00:04:09.878
Let me do one thing,
I'll change this to [4, 7]

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.180
Or I'll change to 8

00:04:11.541 --> 00:04:14.861
And now I want the index of (3)

00:04:15.078 --> 00:04:18.391
Firstly it'll do the 'Slicing' of this
'Tuple' from [4:8]

00:04:18.764 --> 00:04:20.850
Now it is saying " x is not in 'Tuple' "

00:04:20.883 --> 00:04:23.350
In-fact I made a mistake, this (3)
must be added in the starting

00:04:24.049 --> 00:04:25.209
(3) must be added in the starting

00:04:25.417 --> 00:04:28.564
It'll first do slicing of the
'Tuple' as [4:8]

00:04:28.762 --> 00:04:36.040
When it'll do slicing of this 'Tuple' with [4:8]
[0] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7]

00:04:36.065 --> 00:04:37.778
[4:8] will contain this

00:04:38.122 --> 00:04:41.875
So look, what will be the occurrence of (3)?

00:04:42.192 --> 00:04:45.652
What will be the index of (3)?

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.193
It'll be 5
[0] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5]

00:04:49.219 --> 00:04:51.486
In original 'Tuple' 3 is on the fifth index

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:52.956
That's why we got 5

00:04:53.311 --> 00:04:55.858
What if I change this to (31)?

00:04:56.088 --> 00:04:58.908
Then I won't get " 5 " but " 7 "

00:04:59.069 --> 00:05:00.756
Is the output is 7?
Yes it is

00:05:01.295 --> 00:05:03.409
Because it is on 7ᵗʰ index

00:05:03.776 --> 00:05:04.629
Index is 7

00:05:04.722 --> 00:05:07.318
[0] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7]
here's 3

00:05:07.379 --> 00:05:09.339
That's why we'll get 7

00:05:09.701 --> 00:05:13.587
You'll use " .index " method quite a
multiple times in your life

00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:16.931
But you'll rarely use the starting variation

00:05:17.107 --> 00:05:18.293
You'll use this one a lot of times

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:21.194
But again I just told you how it works

00:05:22.001 --> 00:05:25.488
Remember it'll raise value error if the
element is not present in the 'Tuple'

00:05:25.745 --> 00:05:27.771
For eg. If I do something like this....

00:05:27.971 --> 00:05:31.664
If I change this to (311) from (3)

00:05:31.985 --> 00:05:33.332
So it'll raise a value error

00:05:33.411 --> 00:05:37.158
It'll display "There's nothing like (311) in your
'Tuple' , why are you trying to print it? "

00:05:37.555 --> 00:05:40.021
It raise value error as we saw just now

00:05:40.269 --> 00:05:42.279
Then comes " .length " in tuple's methods

00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:47.171
If I want to show you the length of this 'Tuple'

